Post-Viral Systemic Fatigue and Multi-Organ Syndrome
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Profound post-viral fatigue, Post-exertional malaise, Severe brain fog, Normal comprehensive medical evaluations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-042
Reason for Visit: Acute physical collapse, extreme palpitations, and profound cognitive impairment following minor physical exertion, requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute cardiopulmonary decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient who collapsed into bed with severe tachycardia, chest tightness, and an inability to form coherent sentences after simply walking to the kitchen. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate haemodynamic stability, rule out acute arrhythmias or myocardial ischaemia, and ensure safety prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I just walked to the fridge and my heart exploded in my chest. I can't think straight, my whole body aches, and I am completely physically crushed."
History of Present Illness: The patient suffered a documented viral illness 5 months ago. Since then, the patient describes profound, newly onset, and unrelenting generalized fatigue. The timeline demonstrates a chronic state of post-exertional malaise (PEM). The acute crisis today was triggered by a minor physical task (walking to the kitchen), resulting in a severe, disproportionate crash. The patient reports persistent exertional tachycardia, severe palpitations, and vague, diffuse chest tightness unassociated with severe coronary ischaemia. The partner notes severe cognitive impairment ('brain fog'), causing profound difficulty with concentration today. Patient also reports widespread, migrating muscle pain, frequent severe headaches, and chronic, low-grade, non-restorative sleep.
Functional Status: Bedbound, acutely crashed, speaking in very short, whispered sentences to conserve energy.
Social/Safety Concerns: Partner is highly distressed by the suddenness and severity of the physical collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Profoundly exhausted, pale, resting with eyes closed, clearly struggling to engage in the assessment.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals regular tachycardia; no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. Neurological exam reveals an alert patient with significantly delayed verbal processing but no focal motor or sensory deficits. Abdomen is soft and non-tender. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Profound post-viral fatigue, Post-exertional malaise, Severe brain fog, and Normal comprehensive medical evaluations (patient had normal Holter/Echo 2 months ago).
Environmental Observations: Room is extremely dark and quiet, indicating severe sensory hypersensitivity.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Systemic Fatigue Syndrome (Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome phenotype) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute PEM Crash)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chest tightness is vague, the ECG/Echo history is normal, and the symptoms are part of a massive systemic, multi-system crash rather than isolated, severe central crushing pain.

Adrenal Insufficiency (Addisonian Crisis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of profound hypotension, severe vomiting, or hyperpigmentation, though baseline cortisol should ideally be checked by the regular GP.

Primary Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct, measurable physiological trigger (post-exertional malaise/tachycardia) which is the hallmark of this neuroimmune condition, rather than primary anhedonia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a severe acute post-exertional malaise (PEM) "crash" driven by post-viral systemic fatigue syndrome. The abrupt onset of severe tachycardia and chest tightness following minimal exertion is terrifying for the patient and mimics a primary cardiac event. An urgent on-site clinical assessment was strictly required to physically auscultate the heart and lungs, confidently rule out acute life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergencies, and validate the physiological nature of the crash to prevent inappropriate, energy-draining emergency department transfers. This intervention safely manages an acute functional decline and severe distress, strictly satisfying the urgent after-hours criteria (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise immediate, absolute sensory and physical rest (radical rest). Recommend aggressive oral hydration to support blood volume and blunt the reactive tachycardia. Urgent referral back to the regular GP for ongoing management of pacing strategies and to re-verify that basic metabolic panels (including thyroid and iron studies) remain normal.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly implementing 'radical rest'—lying flat in a dark room with a sleep mask and earplugs for 4 hours—the patient will aggressively shut down sensory input; this sensory deprivation will reward the patient by shortening the duration of the severe autonomic crash, reinforcing the use of proactive, extreme rest during flares.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None at this stage.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days to review pacing limits.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops sudden, heavy crushing chest pain radiating to the jaw or arm, experiences a sudden loss of consciousness (fainting), or develops severe, sudden shortness of breath at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; requires ongoing exclusion of other fatiguing illnesses by a regular GP.
Clinical Course: A PEM "crash" indicates that the body's cellular energy production has been temporarily overwhelmed. The acute severe symptoms (tachycardia, profound weakness) will gradually subside with strict, absolute rest over several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your nervous system is currently severely overwhelmed, causing your heart to race. However, if you develop a deep, heavy, crushing pain in the center of your chest, or if you actually pass out, you must call an ambulance immediately to ensure it is not a primary heart issue.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is currently reacting to minor physical activity the way a healthy person's body reacts to running a marathon. This "crash" is a severe physiological exhaustion response. The only immediate treatment is radical, absolute rest to allow your autonomic nervous system to slowly reset.
Actionable Steps: Keep the room completely dark and silent. Hydrate well. Do not attempt to "push through" this fatigue; doing so will actively prolong the severe crash phase.

